      "writtenOn": "2021-06-01T15:35:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "FAULT_STATUS_MASK initializes to 0xFF by default in the TCPCI spec and I don\u0027t see us changing it anywhere, so it should be good.\n\nWeirdly, we\u0027re not enabling the fault interrupt, as seen here: \n  ALERT_MASK                    (0x12) \u003d 0x207f\n\nBut we can still react to the overcurrent during our interrupt handling: \n[731.719168 C0 FAULT 0x08 detected]\n[731.719471 C0: overcurrent!]\n21-06-01 09:25:31.245 [731.720739 C0 FAULT 0x08 handled]\n\nEvidently because it\u0027s also triggering a power status interrupt, where we notice the fault: \n[1128.582136 C0: Alert status 0x0202]\n21-06-01 09:32:06.534 [1128.583140 C0 FAULT 0x08 detected]\n[1128.583554 C0: overcurrent!]\n[1128.585103 C0 FAULT 0x08 handled]\n\nSo we can function this way without enabling FAULT as an interrupt if we assume it\u0027ll go hand-in-hand with the power status change, but I\u0027d be open to enabling the FAULT interrupt either when OCP is supported or when running TCPCI rev 2.0",
      "parentUuid": "716ccb7b_d92e1417",
      "revId": "26043ec1d997483a81e0c8afc6249e02399c6d72",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0a2baf94_5f177fa5",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
